A simplified kernel object database:

Create objects → store them → track them → lookup them → delete them safely → reclaim memory under pressure

Think of it like a mini version of:

inode cache (filesystem)
dentry cache
device registry
kernel object store

When you run:

sudo insmod kobjx_slab_rcu_registry.ko
Step-by-step:
1. SLAB cache is created (kmem_cache_create)
2. Shrinker is registered (for memory reclaim)
3. /proc/kobjx_stats file is created
4. Two objects are created (A and B)
5. They are inserted into:
      - linked list (tracking)
      - hash table (fast lookup)
6. One object is looked up using ID
7. Both objects are deleted using RCU-safe deletion


3. What data structures are used (important)
1. SLAB cache
   → fast allocation/free (kernel allocator layer)

2. Linked List
   → keeps all objects in order

3. Hash Table
   → fast lookup by ID (O(1) average)

4. RCU (Read-Copy Update)
   → safe concurrent deletion without blocking readers

5. Shrinker
   → removes old objects when kernel needs memory

6. Procfs
   → exposes stats to user space (/proc/kobjx_stats)
   
   
