================================================================================
A) OBJECT CREATION (kobjx_alloc)
================================================================================

WHAT IT DOES:
----------------------------------------
Creates a kernel object and registers it into multiple tracking systems.

FLOW:
----------------------------------------
1. Allocate memory from SLAB cache:
      kmem_cache_alloc()

2. Initialize object fields:
      - id
      - name
      - timestamp (jiffies)
      - refcount / atomic state

3. Insert into tracking structures:
      - Linked List (ordered traversal)
      - Hash Table (fast lookup by ID)

4. Update counters:
      - active object count
      - per-CPU allocation stats

WHAT IT SIMULATES IN REAL KERNEL:
----------------------------------------
✔ inode allocation
✔ dentry cache object creation
✔ task_struct-like object initialization

ADDITIONAL TECH INSIGHT:
----------------------------------------
- SLAB keeps objects in CPU caches for speed
- avoids fragmentation vs kmalloc
- constructor (ctor) ensures zeroed + safe state reuse

================================================================================


================================================================================
B) OBJECT LOOKUP (kobjx_lookup)
================================================================================

WHAT IT DOES:
----------------------------------------
Finds an object using its ID using hash table + RCU safety.

FLOW:
----------------------------------------
1. Enter RCU read section:
      rcu_read_lock()

2. Search hash bucket:
      hash_for_each_possible_rcu()

3. Match object ID

4. Return pointer without blocking writers

WHAT IT SIMULATES IN REAL KERNEL:
----------------------------------------
✔ dcache lookup (d_lookup)
✔ inode hash lookup
✔ networking flow cache lookup

ADDITIONAL TECH INSIGHT:
----------------------------------------
- RCU allows lock-free reads
- readers NEVER block writers
- writers defer deletion via call_rcu()

KEY IDEA:
----------------------------------------
READ IS FAST + LOCK-FREE

================================================================================


================================================================================
C) OBJECT DELETION (kobjx_delete)
================================================================================

WHAT IT DOES:
----------------------------------------
Safely removes object from system without breaking readers.

FLOW:
----------------------------------------
1. Remove from shared structures:
      - linked list
      - hash table

2. Decrease active counter

3. Mark object inactive:
      atomic_set(in_use, 0)

4. Schedule safe free:
      call_rcu()

WHAT IT SIMULATES IN REAL KERNEL:
----------------------------------------
✔ file deletion (VFS unlink)
✔ network object teardown
✔ kernel cache eviction

ADDITIONAL TECH INSIGHT:
----------------------------------------
- immediate free is unsafe (other CPUs may read object)
- RCU guarantees grace period completion
- memory freed AFTER all readers finish

================================================================================


================================================================================
D) SHRINKER (scan_objects)
================================================================================

WHAT IT DOES:
----------------------------------------
Reclaims memory under pressure based on object age.

FLOW:
----------------------------------------
1. Kernel memory pressure triggers shrinker

2. Iterate object list

3. Check age:
      if (current_time - created_time > threshold)

4. Remove old objects

5. Free SLAB memory

WHAT IT SIMULATES IN REAL KERNEL:
----------------------------------------
✔ slab shrinkers (kmem_cache shrinker)
✔ page reclaim system (kswapd concept)
✔ inode cache pruning

ADDITIONAL TECH INSIGHT:
----------------------------------------
- kernel uses shrinkers during memory pressure
- aging-based eviction mimics LRU policy
- integrates with VM subsystem

